1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
«أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ
الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ» ('I seek refuge in God from the accursed Satan; in the name of God, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful').

2
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:45,440
This surah — apparently — was revealed in
Medina.

3
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
It is an oath by the war-horses that
served in

4
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,000
the defense of Islam — an oath by these
steeds in

5
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,000
their various states: as they gallop
panting,

6
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
as their hooves strike sparks from the
rock,

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
and as those swift chargers raid the
attacking

8
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
enemy and charge into their midst at dawn,

9
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
raising dust on the battlefield. This oath

10
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
refers back to the jihad of the warriors.
As for

11
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
camels and beasts of burden, they were
mentioned

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
in Surah al-Nahl: «وَالْخَيْلَ
وَالْبِغَالَ

13
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,052
وَالْحَمِيرَ لِتَرْكَبُوهَا» ('and

14
00:02:03,052 --> 00:02:10,052
horses, mules and donkeys, that you may
ride

15
00:02:10,052 --> 00:02:17,052
them,' al-Nahl 16:8) — recalled there as
divine

16
00:02:17,052 --> 00:02:24,052
blessings, not as objects of an oath.
Likewise

17
00:02:24,052 --> 00:02:30,994
of the flocks He said: «وَلَكُمْ فِيهَا

18
00:02:30,994 --> 00:02:37,994
جَمَالٌ حِينَ تُرِيحُونَ وَحِينَ
تَسْرَحُونَ»

19
00:02:37,994 --> 00:02:44,994
('and for you there is beauty in them when

20
00:02:44,994 --> 00:02:51,994
you bring them in and when you send them
out,' al-Nahl

21
00:02:51,994 --> 00:02:58,760
16:6) — this is the beauty of herding.

22
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:07,740
The carrying capacity belongs

23
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:14,740
to the horse, mule and donkey,

24
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:21,740
which have a role in transport;

25
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:27,857
but in a military charge

26
00:03:27,857 --> 00:03:34,857
only the war-horse takes part

27
00:03:34,857 --> 00:03:41,857
— not mule, donkey or ass. So

28
00:03:41,857 --> 00:03:48,857
the oath refers in truth to the

29
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:55,857
jihad of the warriors who run

30
00:03:55,857 --> 00:04:02,739
panting on the battlefield,

31
00:04:02,739 --> 00:04:09,739
whose iron-shod hooves strike

32
00:04:09,739 --> 00:04:16,739
fire from the rock; and when

33
00:04:16,739 --> 00:04:23,739
dawn comes their assault begins,

34
00:04:23,739 --> 00:04:29,092
and the plundering of

35
00:04:29,092 --> 00:04:35,974
the disbelievers is carried

36
00:04:35,974 --> 00:04:42,974
out, raising dust and clamor

37
00:04:42,974 --> 00:04:49,974
as they charge into the enemy host

38
00:04:49,974 --> 00:04:56,601
and rout them. Although it

39
00:04:56,601 --> 00:05:03,483
was also transmitted — from

40
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:10,483
Ibn 'Abbas, via al-Tabari and

41
00:05:10,483 --> 00:05:17,483
later al-Zamakhshari's al-Kashshaf

42
00:05:17,483 --> 00:05:22,071
— that this refers

43
00:05:22,071 --> 00:05:28,953
to the pack-camels carrying

44
00:05:28,953 --> 00:05:35,953
pilgrims' loads from Mecca to

45
00:05:35,953 --> 00:05:42,580
Arafat, then Mash'ar, then

46
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:49,580
Mina and back, what truly befits

47
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:56,207
such an oath is the charge

48
00:05:56,207 --> 00:06:03,207
of the warriors. In the Hajj it

49
00:06:03,207 --> 00:06:10,207
is the lean camels of the deep

50
00:06:10,207 --> 00:06:17,207
ravine (fajj 'amiq) that are

51
00:06:17,207 --> 00:06:24,207
spoken of — not this running,

52
00:06:24,207 --> 00:06:31,207
speed, and striking of sparks;

53
00:06:31,207 --> 00:06:37,840
none of that belongs here.

54
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:45,671
Although in Hajj and 'Umra powerful camels
come from afar,

55
00:06:45,671 --> 00:06:52,671
what the Qur'an relates refers partly to
the lean pilgrim-camels

56
00:06:52,671 --> 00:06:57,840
and partly to the deep ravine (fajj
'amiq).

57
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:04,710
In any case, the mounts other than
pilgrim-camels — in the end, there is the deep

58
00:07:04,710 --> 00:07:10,440
ravine and the pilgrim-camel; but the one
on the battlefield is the war-horse.

59
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:17,420
The point is that although both images
occur, what befits the matter of jihad is what took place in Medina.

60
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:24,300
And it was jihad that was able to
establish Islam.

61
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:31,120
«الإِسْلَامُ يَعْلُو وَلَا يُعْلَى
عَلَيْهِ» ('Islam is exalted and nothing is exalted above it') — a declarative sentence delivered with the intent of a command.

62
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:38,120
This «لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ»
('that

63
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:44,211
He may make it prevail over all religion,'

64
00:07:44,211 --> 00:07:50,882
cited in al-Tawba 9:33, al-Fath 48:28,
al-Saff

65
00:07:50,882 --> 00:07:57,843
61:9) — though outwardly a report — is
delivered

66
00:07:57,843 --> 00:08:03,934
with performative intent. And in the close

67
00:08:03,934 --> 00:08:10,934
of Surah al-Bayyina, that the believers
and God-fearing

68
00:08:10,934 --> 00:08:17,170
are the best of people on earth — the
best,

69
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:22,826
most upright, most learned civilization

70
00:08:22,826 --> 00:08:29,642
— He said «هُمْ خَيْرُ الْبَرِيَّةِ»
('they are

71
00:08:29,642 --> 00:08:36,023
the best of creation,' al-Bayyina 98:7).
The

72
00:08:36,023 --> 00:08:42,984
truly righteous (abrar) are not merely
those who

73
00:08:42,984 --> 00:08:49,945
do good deeds; the man of birr is he whose
heart

74
00:08:49,945 --> 00:08:56,945
is open like the vast plains, unbounded —
who says,

75
00:08:56,945 --> 00:09:03,326
'O God, forgive me and all believing men
and

76
00:09:03,326 --> 00:09:10,287
women in the easts and wests of the
earth,' and,

77
00:09:10,287 --> 00:09:17,287
'It is not sweet for me to rest while in
the farthest

78
00:09:17,287 --> 00:09:23,813
reach of my rule there is an orphan, a
hungry

79
00:09:23,813 --> 00:09:30,780
one, or one in distress.' He is the man of
birr.

80
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:38,478
'Bariyya' means 'the created,' so you
Muslims are the best of creatures

81
00:09:38,478 --> 00:09:44,871
— on condition that a person struggles
against disbelief, colonialism

82
00:09:44,871 --> 00:09:50,708
and tyranny, whether by the pen or by
action. These declarative

83
00:09:50,708 --> 00:09:56,823
sentences are delivered with performative
intent: «أَنْتُمْ خَيْرُ

84
00:09:56,823 --> 00:10:02,382
الْبَرِيَّةِ» ('you are the best of
creation'). It addresses

85
00:10:02,382 --> 00:10:08,500
a universal principle to the listener,
without preface or command.

86
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:15,096
Without «الْعَادِيَاتِ ضَبْحًا،
وَالْمُورِيَاتِ قَدْحًا، فَالْمُغِيرَاتِ صُبْحًا، فَأَثَرْنَ بِهِ نَقْعًا» ('By the chargers panting,

87
00:10:15,096 --> 00:10:20,800
striking sparks, raiding at dawn, raising
therein a cloud of dust,' al-'Adiyat 100:1-4) — without struggle, without jihad — or with them.

88
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:26,172
If it is with these, then it becomes
«خَيْرُ الْبَرِيَّةِ» ('the best of creation')

89
00:10:26,172 --> 00:10:30,760
— the best civilization is yours, the best
culture is yours, the best life is yours.

90
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:34,280
If not, this divine promise is not
realized.

91
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:41,280
In the close of Surah al-Bayyina came
«إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ

92
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:46,472
أُولَٰئِكَ هُمْ خَيْرُ الْبَرِيَّةِ»
('those who believe and

93
00:10:46,472 --> 00:10:53,472
do righteous deeds — they are the best of
creation,' al-Bayyina 98:7). Such statements,

94
00:10:53,472 --> 00:10:59,184
though a promise, carry a definite
command: you must have the best

95
00:10:59,184 --> 00:11:05,848
civilization, nation, law, and leader —
the imam of the community whom He has

96
00:11:05,848 --> 00:11:12,600
appointed — and its measure is the Qur'an
and the Pure Ones (the Ahl al-Bayt).

97
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:20,700
Therefore, where the discourse is about
carrying loads, the discourse is about the horse.

98
00:11:23,700 --> 00:11:28,358
Where it is about combat, the discourse is
only about the horse — the

99
00:11:28,358 --> 00:11:32,680
mule and donkey have no precise part in
it, let alone the camel.

100
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:37,944
After the oaths, God Most Holy reproaches

101
00:11:37,944 --> 00:11:41,620
this human being more than fifty times.

102
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:49,660
The praise, exaltation and honor God gives
the human being go back to his primordial nature

103
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:56,295
(fitra) — to that universal covenant:
«وَإِذْ أَخَذَ رَبُّكَ مِنْ بَنِي آدَمَ مِنْ

104
00:11:56,295 --> 00:12:02,930
ظُهُورِهِمْ ذُرِّيَّتَهُمْ وَأَشْهَدَهُمْ
عَلَىٰ أَنْفُسِهِمْ أَلَسْتُ بِرَبِّكُمْ

105
00:12:02,930 --> 00:12:09,403
قَالُوا بَلَىٰ» ('And when your Lord took
from the loins of the children of Adam

106
00:12:09,403 --> 00:12:16,403
their descendants and made them testify
concerning themselves: Am I not your Lord? They

107
00:12:16,403 --> 00:12:22,960
said: Yes,' al-A'raf 7:172). All virtue
and excellence go back to that dimension.

108
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:29,268
And all flaws and defects go back to
«خُلِقَ الإِنْسَانُ» ('man was created')

109
00:12:29,268 --> 00:12:33,800
— this human being made from clay/mire
(lajin) goes back to that.

110
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:42,500
He said «قَدْ خَابَ مَنْ دَسَّاهَا» ('he
indeed fails who

111
00:12:42,500 --> 00:12:49,500
buries it,' al-Shams 91:10): if someone
buries the divine

112
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:56,024
fitra that testified 'Yes' at the great
covenant,

113
00:12:56,024 --> 00:13:03,024
stops its mouth and will not let it speak,
then his spokesman

114
00:13:03,024 --> 00:13:10,024
becomes his caprice and desire; he moves
by conjecture

115
00:13:10,024 --> 00:13:16,415
and inclination. Even if he outwardly
denies the

116
00:13:16,415 --> 00:13:23,415
Origin, in his own conscience he affirms
it and within

117
00:13:23,415 --> 00:13:30,415
himself he is a witness — for the fitra
cannot be destroyed;

118
00:13:30,415 --> 00:13:36,940
it is alive, only intrigued against and
silenced.

119
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:43,492
It is knowing and aware; it sees, knows,
and can speak; and on the Day of Resurrection the

120
00:13:43,492 --> 00:13:48,594
human being testifies against himself:
«شَاهِدِينَ عَلَىٰ أَنْفُسِهِمْ بِالْكُفْرِ»

121
00:13:48,594 --> 00:13:52,960
('witnessing against themselves that they
disbelieved,' al-Tawba 9:17).

122
00:13:54,260 --> 00:14:01,260
As the limbs bear witness, so does the
fitra against this human — who smothered

123
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:08,260
the fitra and let his animal nature
flourish; who is «لِرَبِّهِ

124
00:14:08,260 --> 00:14:15,260
لَكَنُودٌ» ('ungrateful to his Lord,'
al-'Adiyat 100:6), «وَإِنَّهُ

125
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:22,260
عَلَىٰ ذَٰلِكَ لَشَهِيدٌ» ('and to that he
is a witness,' al-'Adiyat

126
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:28,795
100:7), and «وَإِنَّهُ لِحُبِّ الْخَيْرِ
لَشَدِيدٌ» ('and

127
00:14:28,795 --> 00:14:35,795
violent in his love of wealth,' al-'Adiyat
100:8). 'Wealth' is not

128
00:14:35,795 --> 00:14:42,795
only property: it is rank, position,
leadership. Man uses all that

129
00:14:42,795 --> 00:14:49,795
is on the earth — mineral, plant, animal,
human — as tools. As in Surah

130
00:14:49,795 --> 00:14:56,795
Al 'Imran: «زُيِّنَ لِلنَّاسِ حُبُّ
الشَّهَوَاتِ مِنَ النِّسَاءِ

131
00:14:56,795 --> 00:15:03,560
وَالْبَنِينَ وَالْقَنَاطِيرِ
الْمُقَنْطَرَةِ مِنَ الذَّهَبِ

132
00:15:03,560 --> 00:15:10,560
وَالْفِضَّةِ» ('beautified for people is
the love of desires — of women

133
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:17,560
and sons, and heaped hoards of gold and
silver,' Al 'Imran 3:14).

134
00:15:19,180 --> 00:15:24,872
«وَالْخَيْلِ الْمُسَوَّمَةِ وَالْأَنْعَامِ
وَالْحَرْثِ» ('and

135
00:15:24,872 --> 00:15:30,937
branded horses, and cattle and tillage,'
Al 'Imran 3:14): 'harth'

136
00:15:30,937 --> 00:15:37,282
(tillage) is agriculture; 'branded horses
and cattle' are livestock;

137
00:15:37,282 --> 00:15:42,320
the 'heaped hoards' are gold, silver,
precious objects

138
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:48,480
and antiquities — the mineral; and 'sons
and women' are the human.

139
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:56,800
The heaped hoards pertain to the mineral,
the branded horses and cattle

140
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:03,800
to animal husbandry, tillage to plants,
and 'women and sons' to the human.

141
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:10,687
He presses all four — human, plant,
mineral, animal — into service

142
00:16:10,687 --> 00:16:17,687
as tools. When this person enters the
Resurrection, his first witness

143
00:16:17,687 --> 00:16:24,687
is his fitra: «شَاهِدِينَ عَلَىٰ
أَنْفُسِهِمْ بِالْكُفْرِ» ('witnessing

144
00:16:24,687 --> 00:16:30,740
against themselves that they disbelieved,'
al-Tawba 9:17).

145
00:16:32,060 --> 00:16:35,477
Bear in mind that the witness is other
than the accused.

146
00:16:35,477 --> 00:16:41,640
This self, when its fitra — «قَدْ خَابَ
مَنْ دَسَّاهَا» ('he fails who buries it,' al-Shams 91:10)...

147
00:16:43,480 --> 00:16:50,480
...concealed it and buried it amid
appetites and instincts until it died — yet it is within him, his very self; this same fitra was present at «أَلَسْتُ بِرَبِّكُمْ قَالُوا بَلَىٰ» ('Am I not your Lord?

148
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:57,120
They said: Yes,' al-A'raf 7:172).

149
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:04,120
Now that it has come to this state, this
human who...

150
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:11,120
...is moving upon his animal nature — has
buried his own fitra alive.

151
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:19,851
He has imprisoned his fitra within its own
house; then he himself becomes the

152
00:17:19,851 --> 00:17:26,851
wrongdoer. More than fifty times in the
Noble Qur'an God describes him: ungrateful,

153
00:17:26,851 --> 00:17:32,612
a witness against himself, violent in love
of wealth, a schemer;

154
00:17:32,612 --> 00:17:39,612
he develops problems of belief and of
injustice. «إِنَّ الْمُلُوكَ إِذَا دَخَلُوا

155
00:17:39,612 --> 00:17:45,283
قَرْيَةً أَفْسَدُوهَا وَجَعَلُوا أَعِزَّةَ
أَهْلِهَا أَذِلَّةً»

156
00:17:45,283 --> 00:17:52,034
('when kings enter a town they ruin it and
make its mighty people humbled,'

157
00:17:52,034 --> 00:17:58,965
al-Naml 27:34) is of this kind. These
fifty-odd censures pertain to the empty

158
00:17:58,965 --> 00:18:05,965
half of the human, for he has buried the
full half. On the Day of Resurrection

159
00:18:05,965 --> 00:18:12,716
the first thing to testify against the
deceived human is «شَاهِدِينَ عَلَىٰ

160
00:18:12,716 --> 00:18:19,020
أَنْفُسِهِمْ بِالْكُفْرِ» — this is the
very sentence about the fitra.

161
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:27,140
Some of the great scholars interpreted it
thus: the human who has

162
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:34,140
taken an animal's form — his whole being
testifies against himself

163
00:18:34,140 --> 00:18:41,140
that he is a plunderer; this is a
practical testimony. The first

164
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:48,140
witness is the household witness
(«شَاهِدِينَ عَلَىٰ أَنْفُسِهِمْ»);

165
00:18:48,140 --> 00:18:53,762
then it reaches the limbs: «تَشْهَدُ
عَلَيْهِمْ

166
00:18:53,762 --> 00:19:00,580
أَلْسِنَتُهُمْ بِمَا كَانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ»
('their tongues

167
00:19:00,580 --> 00:19:07,580
testify against them for what they did,'
al-Nur 24:24). Testimony

168
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:14,580
is other than confession: if the criminal
speaks, that is confession;

169
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:20,920
if an outsider speaks against him, that is
testimony.

170
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:27,920
We are other than our hand, foot, skin and
flesh. The hand that gives

171
00:19:27,920 --> 00:19:34,920
or takes bribes over and under the table
is a tool of action.

172
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:42,760
So the hand testifies that this person
took and gave.

173
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:49,063
The Qur'an does not say the hand
confessed; it

174
00:19:49,063 --> 00:19:56,063
says the hand testified — so the self of
the human is

175
00:19:56,063 --> 00:20:03,051
the one who gives and takes; the sin
belongs to the

176
00:20:03,051 --> 00:20:10,051
human, to his empty half, which goes back
to «صَلْصَالٍ

177
00:20:10,051 --> 00:20:15,257
مِنْ حَمَإٍ مَسْنُونٍ» ('sounding clay

178
00:20:15,257 --> 00:20:21,834
of molded mire,' al-Hijr 15:26), while the
other

179
00:20:21,834 --> 00:20:28,685
half goes back to the divine in-breathing
(nafkh).

180
00:20:28,685 --> 00:20:35,536
Testimony may be external, or internal —
the fitra

181
00:20:35,536 --> 00:20:41,565
against nature, or the limbs against
nature.

182
00:20:41,565 --> 00:20:48,279
The nature-bound human who is 'violent in
love of

183
00:20:48,279 --> 00:20:55,279
wealth' must return to «قُلْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ
تُحِبُّونَ

184
00:20:55,279 --> 00:21:00,760
اللهَ فَاتَّبِعُونِي يُحْبِبْكُمُ اللهُ»

185
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:07,760
('Say: if you love God, follow me, and God
will love

186
00:21:07,760 --> 00:21:14,337
you,' Al 'Imran 3:31) — the pre-eternal
Beloved,

187
00:21:14,337 --> 00:21:20,780
who is God; and God said to His beloved:
Say...

188
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:33,000
Though he can become the beloved of God,
he instead loves these four:

189
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:39,720
branded horses, women and sons, heaped
hoards, and tillage.

190
00:21:40,940 --> 00:21:47,488
It is mineral, plant, animal and human,
and he attaches his heart to them. After

191
00:21:47,488 --> 00:21:53,381
smothering his fitra — «قَدْ خَابَ مَنْ
دَسَّاهَا» ('he fails who buries

192
00:21:53,381 --> 00:21:59,274
it,' al-Shams 91:10) — he behaves without
courtesy toward God Most Holy,

193
00:21:59,274 --> 00:22:05,494
becomes ill-mannered and immoral; and his
own faculties testify against him.

194
00:22:05,494 --> 00:22:11,550
This fitra does not die; it endures,
though imprisoned — «وَإِنَّهُ عَلَىٰ

195
00:22:11,550 --> 00:22:17,200
ذَٰلِكَ لَشَهِيدٌ» ('and to that he is a
witness,' al-'Adiyat 100:7).

196
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:24,861
And he loves this wealth intensely. But
the men of God

197
00:22:24,861 --> 00:22:31,402
have surrendered all their wants to God —
giving body,

198
00:22:31,402 --> 00:22:37,822
being cut to pieces, giving their heart's
blood, like

199
00:22:37,822 --> 00:22:44,822
the Master of Martyrs at the Battle of
Uhud (peace be upon

200
00:22:44,822 --> 00:22:51,121
them all). In the inner jihad they chose
the love of

201
00:22:51,121 --> 00:22:58,121
the Beloved over the love of wealth, the
love of the Creator

202
00:22:58,121 --> 00:23:03,693
over the scattered creatures — sons and
women,

203
00:23:03,693 --> 00:23:10,113
heaped hoards, branded horses, tillage —
no; they are

204
00:23:10,113 --> 00:23:16,660
with the love of God. So let us understand
and strive.

205
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:24,976
Let us, as it were, exhume the grave of
the heart — bring out

206
00:23:24,976 --> 00:23:30,868
all its memories, evil deeds, motives,
hypocrisies and

207
00:23:30,868 --> 00:23:37,088
ostentation; and whatever is buried
within, let us revive

208
00:23:37,088 --> 00:23:43,200
it and make it present at the scene of the
Resurrection.

209
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:50,471
The human himself knows that when we
exhume the grave and what is in

210
00:23:50,471 --> 00:23:56,773
the breast comes onto the table, he — who
was aware — says: «مَالِ هَٰذَا

211
00:23:56,773 --> 00:24:02,730
الْكِتَابِ لَا يُغَادِرُ صَغِيرَةً وَلَا
كَبِيرَةً إِلَّا أَحْصَاهَا»

212
00:24:02,730 --> 00:24:07,565
('What is this record that leaves nothing
small or great

213
00:24:07,565 --> 00:24:12,920
but has enumerated it?' al-Kahf 18:49).
They can hide nothing.

214
00:24:14,120 --> 00:24:21,120
He is aware now; the inner witness is
present, though he buried it — «دَسَّاهَا» ('he buried it'). «مَالِ هَٰذَا

215
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:27,654
الْكِتَابِ لَا يُغَادِرُ صَغِيرَةً وَلَا
كَبِيرَةً إِلَّا أَحْصَاهَا» ('What is this record that leaves

216
00:24:27,654 --> 00:24:34,569
nothing small or great but has enumerated
it?' al-Kahf 18:49) — the great ledgers are with God and the divine

217
00:24:34,569 --> 00:24:41,547
administrators; but the ordinary register
that seems 'forgotten' is not lost: «عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ مَا أَحْضَرَتْ»

218
00:24:41,547 --> 00:24:48,020
('a soul will know what it has brought
present,' al-Takwir 81:14) — the human carries it with himself.

219
00:24:49,060 --> 00:24:56,060
As for what another records — the noble
scribes «كِرَامًا كَاتِبِينَ»

220
00:24:56,060 --> 00:25:02,854
('noble writers,' al-Infitar 82:11) also
record it — but «عَلِمَتْ

221
00:25:02,854 --> 00:25:08,928
نَفْسٌ مَا أَحْضَرَتْ» ('a soul will know
what it brought,'

222
00:25:08,928 --> 00:25:15,619
al-Takwir 81:14): an indefinite noun here
yields general meaning,

223
00:25:15,619 --> 00:25:22,207
so 'every soul will know what it has
brought present'; the human

224
00:25:22,207 --> 00:25:29,207
himself will make it present. So although
these verses have been linked

225
00:25:29,207 --> 00:25:35,384
to Hajj and the pilgrim-camels, the
primary reading pertains

226
00:25:35,384 --> 00:25:42,281
to the matters of jihad; and it makes the
human aware that he has a

227
00:25:42,281 --> 00:25:49,080
master of the house who witnesses his
deeds and has recorded them.

228
00:25:50,800 --> 00:25:57,800
You yourself are the witness — your fitra
is the witness; and you yourself are the criminal

229
00:25:57,800 --> 00:26:03,951
— your animal nature is the criminal. If
witness and witnessed-against are

230
00:26:03,951 --> 00:26:10,951
one, that has meaning only as a practical
testimony — as it were, one turned into a donkey

231
00:26:10,951 --> 00:26:17,185
or a dog — whereas verbal testimonies must
be multiple. This blessed surah,

232
00:26:17,185 --> 00:26:24,001
named al-'Adiyat, is now complete. Many of
its subtleties were laid out in earlier

233
00:26:24,001 --> 00:26:30,734
surahs and verses, and the scholarly
opinions return to these points. We hope God

234
00:26:30,734 --> 00:26:37,734
Most Holy grants the Muslim community a
good understanding of what is the good and welfare

235
00:26:37,734 --> 00:26:44,052
of this world and the next, and that they
know many of these injunctions are

236
00:26:44,052 --> 00:26:51,052
declarative sentences delivered with
performative intent — telling us the best people

237
00:26:51,052 --> 00:26:57,540
of the age are the believers: O believers,
strive to be the best of the world.

238
00:26:57,540 --> 00:27:04,540
And He forgives us and you. Peace be upon
you, and the mercy of God and His blessings.
